\title{stow: Durable Managed Local Copies of Remote Files}
\description{
\code{stow} turns a remote file URL into a durable managed local copy. It
downloads the file when needed and reuses a matching copy on later calls,
including across R sessions. Managed local copies live in a fixed \code{stow}
subdirectory beneath a platform-appropriate, package-specific user data
directory, so they do not depend on the current working directory or a
package installation.
}
\details{
The default package name, \code{"stow"}, works for direct use. A package that uses
\code{stow} supplies its own package name so that its managed local copies remain
separate from those owned by other packages.
}
\section{Main functions}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=stow]{stow()}} downloads a file or returns a matching managed local copy.
\item \code{\link[=stow_path]{stow_path()}} locates or creates the managed local copy directory.
\item \code{\link[=stow_info]{stow_info()}} lists managed local copies.
\item \code{\link[=stow_prune]{stow_prune()}} removes outdated variants and orphaned internal files while
retaining current usable entries.
\item \code{\link[=stow_remove]{stow_remove()}} explicitly removes all managed local copies for one URL.
}
}

\section{Versions, validation, and durability}{

ETags can distinguish versions served from the same URL, and offline mode
can reuse a managed local copy without a network request from \code{stow()}.
Earlier variants remain available until they are explicitly managed with
\code{\link[=stow_prune]{stow_prune()}} or \code{\link[=stow_remove]{stow_remove()}}.
Downloads are staged before they become managed local copies. An optional
validator can reject both existing and newly downloaded content; a failed
download or a new file that fails validation is never committed.
}

